SQL Injections
------------------------------
    Code Injections in GENERAL
        - Its basically adding your own code into a data stream
        - Mostly due to bad programming
        - So many types (HTML,SQl,XML,LDAP,etc)

    SQL Injections
    ------------------------------
        Its Injecting SQL code into a application accepting input.

        Applications should not be allowing this..

        Can be often executed in the web browser if the client isn't sanitizing the frontend
            - Often you can inject in form or fields.

        Building a SQL injection
        ------------------------------
            SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + userName + "'";

            You could add your own completion via ' OR '1'='1
            
                This could help you grab stuff you wouldn't otherise be able to grab from the database.

-------------------------------
Cross-site Scripting Injection
-------------------------------

    XXS
        originally called cross-site because of browser security flaws
            - information from one site could be shared with another.
        One of the most common web app Vulnerabilities
            -takes advantage of the trust a user has for a site.
        
        XSS commonly use Javascript

    Eg: High level
        
        1 Option
            1. Attacker sends a link containing a malicious script to a victim
                via email or text,etc
            2. Victim will click the link and visit a legitimate site
            
            3. The legitimate site loads in the victims browser,
            malicious script is loaded along with it.

            4. Usually the user can't see it run, but..
            behind the scenes the script might have sent the
            victm's data (session cookies,etc) to attacker.

    Types:
        Non-persistent(reflected) XSS attack
        
            - Web site allows scripts to run in user input.

            - An attacker could take advantage of this by sending a email link that runs a script that 
            in addition to the correct redirect will run a bit of code that sends credentials to the attacker.

            - The script is often embedded into the URL link and executes in the victim's browser.

            - Attacker uses credentails/session IDs/cookies to steal victim's information without their knowledge

            - very sneaky

        eg: In the userput we would add:

             "><script>alert("Your session information" + document.cookie)</script>

             ** NOTE ** within the URL link theres usually a parameter we would add this to within the URL
             to make it run as they click it, if you just add it to the browser it only runs on your system lol

            Eg: https://security.com/index.html?usersearch="><script>alert("Your session information" + document.cookie)</script>

            This would be a good payload to give somone!

        Persistent(stored) XSS attack
            
            - Web sites dont properly sanitize userinput causing javascript to be able to slip through,
            once slipped through any time the database needs that information it will try to grab that data and it
            will be the malicious script and if it is allowed to run on the browser it will run.

            This effects Every user, It really just depends on whether they end up grabbing the data.

            a Forum would grab users posts and if one of those posts have malicious js and is allowed to run, any user
            who runs that forum will get effected, becuase of the nature of grabbing stuff off the database.
        

    Protecting against XSS as a user

        - Be careful when clicking untrusted links.
        - Consider disabling javascript
            - meh
        -keep your browser and applications updated
            - Avoid the nasty browser Vulnerabilities
        
        -as a dev
            - Validate input and dont allow users to add scripts to an input field
